---
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro'
import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro'
import { starCount } from '../lib/stars'

const stars = await starCount()
---

<TextPage
  title="About the project | SpreadPaper"
  description="Who makes SpreadPaper, why it is free, what happens to it long term, and the reasons you can check rather than take on trust."
  socialDescription="One person, one app, MIT licensed, no company and nothing to sell."
  eyebrow="Project"
  heading="One person, no company, nothing to sell"
  lead="SpreadPaper is a personal project given away under the MIT licence. That shapes what you can expect from it, so here is the whole picture rather than a marketing version of it."
>
  <Prose>
    <h2>Who</h2>
    <p>
      Robin van Baalen, a developer in the Netherlands. I also co-founded <a href="https://ganttify.com">Ganttify</a>, which is the day job, and write at <a href="https://robinvanbaalen.nl">robinvanbaalen.nl</a>. SpreadPaper is not a company product and there is no team behind it.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      I have three monitors, and macOS gives every one of them the same wallpaper. A wide photograph becomes three copies of itself instead of one picture. The apps that solved it were paid and closed, so in November 2025 I wrote something that did the one thing I wanted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Almost everything it has grown since then came from other people. Bezel compensation, the presets, the schedules: each one started as someone opening an issue. That is genuinely the development process, not a line on a page.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it is free</h2>
    <p>
      There is no business model, no free tier waiting to become a paid one, and no plan to add one. It costs nothing to run: the app does the work on your Mac, the site is static, and the downloads are hosted by GitHub. Nothing about it needs revenue to keep existing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The one cost it does not carry is an Apple Developer ID, at 99 dollars a year, which is why macOS warns you on first launch. The <a href="/help">help page</a> covers that.
    </p>

    <h2>What you can check</h2>
    <p>
      None of the above has to be taken on trust, which is the point of it being open source.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The source is at <a href="https://github.com/spreadpaper/SpreadPaper"
          >github.com/spreadpaper/SpreadPaper</a
        >, currently {stars} stars, under the MIT licence.
      </li>
      <li>Every release is built from that source by GitHub Actions, in the open.</li>
      <li>
        The app makes exactly one network request, to check for updates. The
        <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> names the file it lives in and how to block it.
      </li>
      <li>Nothing is collected, so there is no data to ask about, sell or lose.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What to expect of it</h2>
    <p>
      It is maintained in evenings and weekends by one person. In practice that means issues get read and usually answered, good ideas tend to ship, and there is no promised response time because promising one would be a lie.
    </p>
    <p>
      If I ever stop working on it, the MIT licence means anyone can pick it up. A fork is welcome. The only ask is that a fork uses its own name, so nobody downloads a build expecting this one. That is in the <a href="/terms">terms</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting in touch</h2>
    <p>
      Ideas and bugs are best as <a href="https://github.com/spreadpaper/SpreadPaper/issues">GitHub issues</a>, because they stay public and other people find them. Anything else reaches me at <a href="mailto:hello@spreadpaper.app">hello@spreadpaper.app</a>.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</TextPage>
